#{[17592186401381 #edb/ref 1080 "n\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4bb8a9be-6f0a-565b-b6b2-11bc4789ac9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401382 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70995bca-c45d-5efe-9c86-d0ad7a6058b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401383 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50f624a9-d806-5717-a7e5-2f6fcc28ebd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401384 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403011 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/47/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb0ef12c-9b61-582b-acd5-7493f7def056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403011 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186403015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403012 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "606ee288-277b-5ad8-a08e-806ce45db66b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403013 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403013 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403013 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30d03c60-3151-59b6-b7b9-fd72aa928ebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403014 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}{sin}^{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403014 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403014 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f900a01-8dad-54ab-bb5e-4a84ad557187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403015 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403015 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403015 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a782534b-97b6-5072-b2d6-441797eca761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403016 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403016 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403016 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8cc1f314-0209-5118-9c75-ea26340d8758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403017 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403017 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403017 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "704fb728-1e9d-5aae-b2b0-2c48902cd828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403947 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,z=c.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $z$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $c$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & =\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}c \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{I}_{n}}}}z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and right to left, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "863bd08e-01cd-5a69-b3ce-bacc176f8b4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403947 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,z=c$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & ={D}^{-1}c \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186403949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403948 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4cf7d7a2-cf8f-5d79-a5a3-2c7fb6abb055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403949 #edb/ref 1080 "${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403949 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403949 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53d8f566-865b-5d1d-968f-ffc7aeaaf0b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403950 #edb/ref 1080 "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403950 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403950 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6898f8cf-ef60-599d-b703-aeb35e14e141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403951 #edb/ref 1080 "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403951 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403951 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0329ed6f-dee9-5205-b238-18984ed73972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403952 #edb/ref 1080 "${D}^{-1}$ and ${D}^{-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403952 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403952 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a15466de-3c65-5970-8e36-5b25e21ca209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403953 #edb/ref 1080 "$D$ and $z$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403953 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403953 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "daabc2d4-f4ce-50f8-9f41-a9e7585ac6e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403954 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,t=q.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $t$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $q$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{\\mathbf{q}}}} \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ecf8e0a5-c1dd-5243-9116-de59a516e7ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403954 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,t=q$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1